WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.400
Absolutely.

2
00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:02.840
I mean, if you talked 10 years ago

3
00:00:02.840 --> 00:00:04.040
to people that traveled a lot,

4
00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:05.360
usually their behavior was,

5
00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:06.840
you get to where you're going,

6
00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:08.340
you get outside, you go for a walk,

7
00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:10.660
you do something physical, and you eat, right.

8
00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:13.620
And so what science is telling us now is,

9
00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:15.700
you know, they figured it out

10
00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:16.740
without knowing why.

11
00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:19.400
And so what we do,

12
00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:21.280
there are studies in which if, you know,

13
00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:24.880
so you by exercising, again,

14
00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:26.580
and by sort of scheduling your feeding

15
00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:27.620
and thinking about that,

16
00:00:27.620 --> 00:00:31.280
you will accelerate the jet lag of the transition

17
00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:33.840
in which your clocks are misaligned.

18
00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:36.800
So you'll get on to a more aligned status,

19
00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:38.500
you'll feel better, faster.

20
00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:41.360
So there are data sets out there

21
00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:42.480
that support the idea

22
00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:44.760
that there are things we can do behaviorally

23
00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:46.380
when we get into a new time zone,

24
00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:49.040
that will improve or speed up

25
00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:51.600
how long we're stuck in that jet lag,

26
00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:53.050
because jet lag is large.

27
00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:53.940
I mean, literally

28
00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:55.900
that is circadian misalignment.

29
00:00:55.900 --> 00:00:57.480
That's why you feel crummy.

30
00:00:57.480 --> 00:00:59.930
It's just your clocks are out of whack.
